# PhraseRoots

PhraseRoots transcribes Thai audio with faster-whisper and translates the
transcript through OpenRouter using Qwen3 30B. Ollama is not required.

## Setup

From `backend/`:

```bash
uv sync
cp .env.example .env
uv run uvicorn app.main:app --reload
```

Set `OPENROUTER_API_KEY` in `.env` after creating a key at OpenRouter. Do not
add API keys to the repository or Vercel frontend environment variables.

Configure the UI login at the same time. The API refuses protected requests
until all three values are set. Generate the secret with `openssl rand -hex 32`.

```env
AUTH_USERNAME=your-username
AUTH_PASSWORD=a-long-unique-password
JWT_SECRET=a-random-64-character-secret
JWT_EXPIRE_MINUTES=480
```

## Web UI

The Vite React UI lives in `frontend/`. It can record audio in the browser,
download a local copy, and translate typed conversations through the backend.

Start it locally in a second terminal:

```bash
cd frontend
cp .env.example .env
npm install
npm run dev
```

Set `VITE_API_URL` in `frontend/.env` to the address of the FastAPI service:

```env
VITE_API_URL=http://127.0.0.1:8000
```

Open the URL printed by Vite, normally `http://localhost:5173`. The browser
will ask for microphone permission when you start a recording. Recordings are
downloaded as `.webm` files when **Download to this device** is selected.

### Save recordings to your Google Drive

PhraseRoots does not use a database for recordings. The **Save in my Google
Drive** option sends audio to `POST /recordings`; the backend uploads it to one
private Google Drive folder and deletes its temporary local copy.

1. Create a `PhraseRoots Recordings` folder in Google Drive and copy its folder
   ID from the URL.
2. In Google Cloud, enable the Google Drive API.
3. Authenticate the local backend using one of the following methods:

   ```sh
   gcloud auth application-default login --scopes=https://www.googleapis.com/auth/drive.file,https://www.googleapis.com/auth/drive.metadata.readonly
   ```

   Sign in with the Google account that owns or can edit the folder. This is the
   preferred approach when organisation policy prevents service-account key
   creation.

   Alternatively, create a service-account JSON key, share the Drive folder
   with its service account email as **Editor**, and store the key outside the
   repository.
4. Add the folder ID to `backend/.env`; only set the key path when using a
   service account:

```env
GOOGLE_DRIVE_FOLDER_ID=your-google-drive-folder-id
GOOGLE_SERVICE_ACCOUNT_FILE=
```

Never commit the JSON key. Google Drive is the permanent recording library;
this version does not create a database or server-side recording archive.

### Deploy backend to Cloud Run

The `backend/Dockerfile` runs CPU `faster-whisper` on Linux with FFmpeg. The
first audio request on each new Cloud Run instance downloads the configured
Whisper model, so text translation is immediately available while audio
translation may have a cold start.

1. Select the target GCP project and enable deployment services. Replace the
placeholders with your own values:

```sh
export PROJECT_ID=YOUR_PROJECT_ID
export REGION=YOUR_REGION
export SERVICE_NAME=phraseroots-api
export SERVICE_ACCOUNT_NAME=phraseroots-api
export SERVICE_ACCOUNT="$SERVICE_ACCOUNT_NAME@$PROJECT_ID.iam.gserviceaccount.com"

gcloud config set project "$PROJECT_ID"
gcloud services enable run.googleapis.com cloudbuild.googleapis.com artifactregistry.googleapis.com secretmanager.googleapis.com
```

2. Create a runtime service account and allow it to read the OpenRouter secret:

```sh
gcloud iam service-accounts create "$SERVICE_ACCOUNT_NAME" --display-name="PhraseRoots API"
gcloud secrets create openrouter-api-key --replication-policy=automatic
gcloud secrets versions add openrouter-api-key --data-file=-
gcloud secrets add-iam-policy-binding openrouter-api-key --member="serviceAccount:$SERVICE_ACCOUNT" --role="roles/secretmanager.secretAccessor"
```

Paste the OpenRouter API key into the third command, then press Control-D. Never
put this key in Vercel or commit it to the repository.

3. Create a `PhraseRoots Recordings` folder in Google Drive and share it as
**Editor** with the service-account email stored in `$SERVICE_ACCOUNT`.
Enable the Google Drive API if it is not enabled and copy the folder ID from
its URL.

4. Deploy from the repository root. Replace `DRIVE_FOLDER_ID` before running:

```sh
gcloud run deploy "$SERVICE_NAME" \
  --source backend \
  --region "$REGION" \
  --service-account "$SERVICE_ACCOUNT" \
  --allow-unauthenticated \
    --set-secrets OPENROUTER_API_KEY=openrouter-api-key:latest,AUTH_PASSWORD=auth-password:latest,JWT_SECRET=jwt-secret:latest \
   --set-env-vars AUTH_USERNAME=YOUR_USERNAME,GOOGLE_DRIVE_FOLDER_ID=DRIVE_FOLDER_ID,ALLOWED_ORIGINS=http://localhost:5173,WHISPER_MODEL=small,WHISPER_COMPUTE_TYPE=int8 \
  --memory 2Gi --cpu 2 --timeout 300
```

5. Copy the service URL returned by the command and verify it:

```sh
curl https://YOUR_CLOUD_RUN_URL/health
```

### Deploy UI to Vercel

1. Import this repository in Vercel and set the project root directory to
`frontend`.
2. Add the `VITE_API_URL` environment variable for Production, Preview, and
Development. Its value is the Cloud Run URL from the previous section, without
a trailing slash.
3. Deploy. Vercel supplies the final `https://YOUR_PROJECT.vercel.app` URL.
4. Update Cloud Run CORS with that URL, then test the production UI:

```sh
gcloud run services update "$SERVICE_NAME" \
  --region "$REGION" \
  --update-env-vars ALLOWED_ORIGINS=https://YOUR_PROJECT.vercel.app
```

Do not set `OPENROUTER_API_KEY` in Vercel: it belongs only on the backend.

### Continuous deployment

Pushes to `main` deploy backend changes to Cloud Run and frontend changes to
Vercel through the workflows in `.github/workflows/`. They use the production
GitHub environment, so configure its required secrets and variables before
pushing deployment changes.

For Cloud Run, use Workload Identity Federation rather than a downloaded
service-account key. Create a GitHub OIDC provider restricted to this
repository and a dedicated deployer service account. Grant that account:

```text
roles/run.admin on the project
roles/cloudbuild.builds.editor on the project
roles/iam.serviceAccountUser on the Cloud Run runtime service account
```

Add these GitHub repository variables:

```text
GCP_PROJECT_ID=YOUR_PROJECT_ID
GCP_REGION=YOUR_REGION
GCP_CLOUD_RUN_SERVICE=YOUR_CLOUD_RUN_SERVICE
GCP_WORKLOAD_IDENTITY_PROVIDER=projects/PROJECT_NUMBER/locations/global/workloadIdentityPools/POOL_ID/providers/PROVIDER_ID
GCP_DEPLOYER_SERVICE_ACCOUNT=DEPLOYER_SERVICE_ACCOUNT_EMAIL
```

Add these GitHub repository secrets for Vercel:

```text
VERCEL_TOKEN
VERCEL_ORG_ID
VERCEL_PROJECT_ID
```

Create `VERCEL_TOKEN` in the Vercel account settings. Obtain the organization
and project IDs from the Vercel project settings. Keep `VITE_API_URL` in the
Vercel project environment settings, not in GitHub Actions; the workflow pulls
it during the production build.

## API

Check the service:

```bash
curl http://127.0.0.1:8000/health
```

Translate Thai text:

```bash
curl -X POST http://127.0.0.1:8000/auth/login \
  -H 'Content-Type: application/json' \
  -d '{"username":"YOUR_USERNAME","password":"YOUR_PASSWORD"}'
```

Use the returned `access_token` as `TOKEN` for every API endpoint other than
`/health` and `/auth/login`:

```bash
curl -X POST http://127.0.0.1:8000/translate \
  -H "Authorization: Bearer $TOKEN" \
  -H 'Content-Type: application/json' \
  -d '{"text":"สวัสดีครับ"}'
```

Transcribe and translate an audio recording:

```bash
curl -X POST http://127.0.0.1:8000/translate/audio \
  -H "Authorization: Bearer $TOKEN" \
  -F 'audio=@/absolute/path/to/recording.m4a'
```

OpenRouter receives transcript input only through the backend. This setup is
intended for a trusted personal backend, not a public multi-user API.
